A||N||15||BigTurbo87GT||Fabricate a flywheel ?||||1||
Z||000000||BigTurbo87GT||05-01-2001||06:36 PM||||Anybody out there have the ability and equipment to fabricate a flywheel for me?<BR>I think I have a source for one but I need to start checking for alternative sources just in case.<P>Need:<BR>Flywheel for 5 Speed Getrag/Northstar motor combination<P>Want:<BR>Fabricated from Aluminum, not a plugged and re-drilled iron piece.<BR>Thanks for any leads and/or help,  Bill<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||05-02-2001||07:40 AM||||If I remember correctly, one of the vendors on this list sells aluminum flywheels. I think it's designed to be a stock replacement but the may be able to modify the specifications to bolt to a N* crank. If not, maybe they will sell you a blank and you can have it drilled locally. Do a search on "aluminum flywheels" and see what turns up.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000002||Will||05-02-2001||09:28 AM||||What are you planning on using for electronics for this swap? As you may have found, I'm working on the same thing.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 05-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||BigTurbo87GT||05-02-2001||10:48 AM||||Thanks guys for your input. I actually got an Email from George at the Fiero Shop saying he could fab whatever I wanted so I may now have what I need.<P>ECMs:<BR>I haven't decided for sure yet but I'm thinking of going with a complete wiring harness and ECM from CHR Fab OR a stock computer with a harness from Howell. The stock computer seems to have issues with the limp home mode kicking in. Apparently since it can't find all the pieces of the drive train and chassis it goes gimpy, so I'm leaning towards CHR Fab unit.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||BigTurbo87GT||05-02-2001||10:56 AM||||Hey Will, how far along are you?  I just trial bolted my tranny to the engine last night. I found that the Northstar doesn't have all the holes threaded and one of them doesn't line up at all! Not that big of a deal, I think I can thread the ones that aren't already and the missing one won't be a problem. Fortunately the input shaft on the tranny lines up perfectly with the pilot hole in the crank. Did you have this situation with your combination? My engine is a 96 my tranny is an 87 Getrag.<P>I haven't looked at mounting the engine on the frame yet, I'll probably start looking at that this weekend, what are your thoughts there?<BR>Thanks for sharing! This is gonna be a really fun project.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||05-02-2001||11:23 AM||||C Shelby used the olds aurura with a stick shift in his new S-1 car what flywheel and F. I. and spark controlers were used in it??<BR>production even if limited is cheaper than custom work. what does Shelby use???||||reg||1
Z||000006||BigTurbo87GT||05-02-2001||11:57 AM||||I did a quick search on the web and didn't find any useful info on the Shelby S1 other than generic specs. If anyone has any idea who to contact to get this information I would pursue this route. However, CHR Fab, Electromotive (Tec II) and the like are around $1300 so I doubt that Shelby would sell a setup for much, if any, less.<BR>CHR Fab has an aluminum flywheel for a rear wheel drive 5 speed (from an S10) but I don't know that they are the same flywheel.<P>Does anybody know if the S10 5 speed shares flywheel specs with the Getrag 5 Speed? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||05-02-2001||01:16 PM||||Big,<P>I suppose you asking about the early model S10s that used the 2.8. A quick call to you local GM parts guy may be a big help. <P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||pherder||05-02-2001||02:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BigTurbo87GT:<BR><B><BR>ECMs:<BR>I haven't decided for sure yet but I'm thinking of going with a complete wiring harness and ECM from CHR Fab OR a stock computer with a harness from Howell. The stock computer seems to have issues with the limp home mode kicking in. Apparently since it can't find all the pieces of the drive train and chassis it goes gimpy, so I'm leaning towards CHR Fab unit.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I have just what you need ... a fully programmable ECU ... <P>See  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/003204.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/003204.html</A> <P>From the manual:<BR>Up to 16,000 rpm.<BR>It will work on 1 to 12 cylinders.<BR>Normally aspirated or supercharged (up to 30 psi!)<BR>Multipoint injection.<BR>Distributed ignition systems or direct fire systems with 1 to 4 coils.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000009||BigTurbo87GT||05-02-2001||02:33 PM||||I talked to Haltech first because they were the most affordable. Unfortunately they don't support Sequential Injection (the Cadillac is sequentially injected).||||reg||1
Z||000010||pherder||05-02-2001||03:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BigTurbo87GT:<BR><B>I talked to Haltech first because they were the most affordable. Unfortunately they don't support Sequential Injection (the Cadillac is sequentially injected).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes the <B>STOCK</B> ECM runs the injectors sequentially.  It doesn't keep you from running them batch with another computer.  Sequential would be preferred but if you can't get the stock ecm to work other than limp home mode, what's the difference if you go back to batch and tune it to run from there?<P>Paul<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||05-02-2001||04:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BigTurbo87GT:<BR><B>Hey Will, how far along are you?  I just trial bolted my tranny to the engine last night. I found that the Northstar doesn't have all the holes threaded and one of them doesn't line up at all! Not that big of a deal, I think I can thread the ones that aren't already and the missing one won't be a problem. Fortunately the input shaft on the tranny lines up perfectly with the pilot hole in the crank. Did you have this situation with your combination? My engine is a 96 my tranny is an 87 Getrag.<P>I haven't looked at mounting the engine on the frame yet, I'll probably start looking at that this weekend, what are your thoughts there?<BR>Thanks for sharing! This is gonna be a really fun project.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I haven't put the trans up to the engine yet. I'm waiting for my dad to get the jag out of the garage.<P>Which hole doesn't line up? On my engine ('95) the middle hole on the left isn't there (too close to the water jacket, I guess).<P>I was thinking of using the fiero trans mount locations, and fabbing a bracket that would pick up some N* timing cover bolts and send a foot down to the stock fiero engine mount. I was also going to use the left N* engine mount bracket and mount attached to the forward transverse cradle rail. I want to use a dogbone, but I'll have to get the engine in to figure out exactly where.<P>I have a few esoteric ideas for electronics, but I'll hold my tongue until I know more.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BigTurbo87GT||05-02-2001||05:18 PM||||Looking from the back (v6 starter mount on the right) the last tranny bolt on the right is hanging out in the air with no motor behind it. All the other holes line up perfectly. Of the remaining holes though, only 3 are threaded.<P>The only modifications that need to be made are grinding a little notch out of the coolant crossover for clutch arm travel and grinding off the tab on the top right of the tranny (it was too tall to fit under the coolant crossover). Then on the inside of the tranny some of the webbing has to be removed to fit the starter.<BR>            p &lt;-- tab cut off<BR>         o---o<BR>        /     \<BR>  --&gt;  O       O   &lt;-- These aren't threaded<BR>      /         \<BR>     o          () &lt;-- This one doesn't line up<P>At some point a cover will have to be made to keep dirt and water from getting into the clutch as the engine and tranny leave a lot of open space.||||reg||1
Z||000013||rockcrawl||05-02-2001||08:58 PM|||| I have contacted Holley tech support about this and this is what they told me. The Comander 950 will run the Northstar with sequential injection. It will require a separate spark controller such as Holley's Annihilator. It is also cheaper than a Haltech.||||reg||1
Z||000014||BigTurbo87GT||05-03-2001||11:45 AM||||Yeah, the Holley unit is what CHR Fab packages with their harness. They do the basic programming and your ready to go.<BR>Pretty sweet deal.||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||05-03-2001||07:09 PM||||You can buy the 950 and harness for half of what chrfab sells it for. Download the base maps from Holley's website to get started and tune from there.||||reg||1
